<h1>Advanced Configuration Parameters group</h1>
<p>The <strong>Advanced Configuration Parameters</strong> group demonstrates configuration that is generated or
	evaluated at runtime. Its tests fetch choices from external sources, generate test cases from matrices of
	values, exclude invalid combinations, randomize dimension values and case order, and populate matrix
	dimensions through functions. These features go beyond choosing or entering the fixed values covered in the
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/basic-configuration-parameters-group/">Basic Configuration Parameters
	group</a>.</p>

<h2 id="lambda-selector-parameter">Lambda Selector Parameter</h2>
<p>Earlier Maze tests require a manually downloaded application and the folder containing its executable. This
	test replaces that setup with a version selector. Selecting a Maze app release from GitHub downloads the
	matching binary in the background or reuses its cached copy.</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/advanced-configuration-parameters-group/lambda-selector-parameter-test.png", "Lambda Selector Parameter test selected under the Advanced Configuration Parameters group, showing an empty Maze App Version field with its selection button", "large") }}

<h3 id="selecting-a-version">Selecting a value</h3>
<p>Select the button at the right of <strong>Maze App Version</strong> to open its dialog. Leave the filter empty
	to request every available release, or enter part of a tag such as <code>v3</code>, then select
	<strong>Fetch</strong>. The returned release tags populate the drop-down:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/advanced-configuration-parameters-group/selection-drop-box.png", "Maze App Version selection dialog after fetching releases, with Maze-v3.0.0, Maze-v2.0.0 and Maze-v1.0.0 shown in the open drop-down", "large") }}
<p>Choose one version and select <strong>OK</strong> to assign its tag to the test configuration:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/advanced-configuration-parameters-group/selected-version.png", "Lambda Selector Parameter configuration after confirming the selection, with maze-v3.0.0 assigned to Maze App Version", "large") }}
<p>Fetching again replaces the available choices with the callback's latest result while preserving the current
	selection when that value is still available.</p>

<h3 id="config-field">Defining a dynamic selector</h3>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_advanced_configuration_parameters/test_lambda_selector_parameter/") }}

<p>A regular <code>OrcraftConfigField.Select</code> receives its choices through <code>options</code> when the
	configuration class is defined. <code>OrcraftConfigField.SelectLambda</code> instead receives an
	<code>options_lambda</code> callback that the controller invokes when options are requested. This mechanism
	supports values obtained from external or changing sources.</p>

<p>The test developer declares a <code>SelectLambda</code> field in the test's configuration class and assigns
	its provider callback through <code>options_lambda</code>:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('maze_app_version = OrcraftConfigField.SelectLambda(
	default="",
	label="Maze App Version",
	input_placeholder="Filter versions (e.g. v3)",
	button_text="Fetch",
	options_lambda=lambda text: fetch_maze_app_release_versions(text),
	layout=OrcraftConfigField.Layout.NewRow,
)') }}
<p>The field starts empty. <code>input_placeholder</code> describes the optional filter accepted by the callback,
  while <code>button_text</code> labels the action that invokes it. The lambda receives the text entered in the
  dialog and returns the values supplied by <code>fetch_maze_app_release_versions</code>.</p>

<h3 id="versions-provider">Supplying dynamic options</h3>
<p>The test developer implements the option-provider function separately from the configuration field. The
	function may read from any source appropriate to the test. In this tutorial, the provider calls a shared
	GitHub helper that requests the Maze app releases, excludes drafts, and returns the release tags as a tuple.
	When filter text is present, the provider returns only tags containing that text:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('def fetch_github_release_versions(owner: str, repo: str, filter_text: str = "") -> tuple[str, ...]:
	url = f"https://api.github.com/repos/{owner}/{repo}/releases"
	request = Request(url, headers=_gh_headers())
	try:
		with urlopen(request, timeout=8) as response:
			releases = json.loads(response.read().decode("utf-8"))
	except Exception:
		return tuple()

	versions = [release["tag_name"] for release in releases if not release.get("draft")]
	query = filter_text.strip().lower()
	if query:
		versions = [version for version in versions if query in version.lower()]
	return tuple(versions)


def fetch_maze_app_release_versions(filter_text: str = "") -> tuple[str, ...]:
	return fetch_github_release_versions(MAZE_APP_OWNER, MAZE_APP_REPO, filter_text)') }}
<p>The constants passed by the wrapper identify the <code>GMCoreAI/orcraft-maze-app</code> repository. Keeping
	the repository-specific wrapper separate allows the generic helper to serve other GitHub-backed selectors.</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/common/github_repo_access.py") }}

<h3 id="using-the-selected-version">Reading the selected value</h3>
{{ codeblock.block('def _run_case(self, case: OrcraftTestCase):

	maze_app = MazeApp.from_release(
		self.config.maze_app_version,
		self.config.command,
		force=self.config.force_redownload,
	)

	maze_app.run()

	return case.result') }}
<p>At run time, the selected release tag is passed to <code>MazeApp.from_release</code>. The helper downloads the
  matching binary for the current platform, or reuses its cached copy unless
  <strong>Re-download (ignore cached binary)</strong> is enabled, then runs the selected command.</p>

<h2 id="lambda-multi-selector-parameter">Lambda Multi-Selector Parameter</h2>
<p><strong>Lambda Multi-Selector Parameter</strong> follows the same principle but supports several values from
	the callback. This test selects multiple Maze app releases and runs the same command against every selected
	version.</p>

<h3 id="selecting-multiple-versions">Selecting multiple values</h3>
<p>Open <strong>Maze App Versions</strong> and select <strong>Fetch</strong>, as in the single-value selector. The
	same callback returns the available release tags, while this dialog displays a checkbox beside each option.
	Select each version to include, then select <strong>OK</strong>:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/advanced-configuration-parameters-group/lambda-multi-selector-parameter.png", "Maze App Versions dialog for Lambda Multi-Selector Parameter, with Maze-v3.0.0 and Maze-v1.0.0 selected and Maze-v2.0.0 unselected", "large") }}
<p>The selected tags are stored together in the <strong>Maze App Versions</strong> field:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/advanced-configuration-parameters-group/selected-versions.png", "Lambda Multi-Selector Parameter configuration with maze-v3.0.0 and maze-v1.0.0 assigned to Maze App Versions", "large") }}
<p>When the test runs, each selected release is downloaded in the background or loaded from the cache. This
	workflow eliminates manual binary downloads and folder-list configuration.</p>

<h3 id="defining-a-dynamic-multi-selector">Defining a dynamic multi-selector</h3>
<p>The implementation differs from the single-value selector in two respects: the configuration uses
	<code>MultiSelectLambda</code> with a tuple default, and the test iterates over the selected values.</p>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_advanced_configuration_parameters/test_lambda_multi_selector_parameter/") }}

{{ codeblock.block('maze_app_versions = OrcraftConfigField.MultiSelectLambda(
	default=(),
	label="Maze App Versions",
	input_placeholder="Filter versions (e.g. v3)",
	button_text="Fetch",
	options_lambda=lambda text: fetch_maze_app_release_versions(text),
	required=True,
	layout=OrcraftConfigField.Layout.NewRow,
)') }}
<p><code>required=True</code> prevents the test from running until at least one version is selected. The field
	reuses the same <code>fetch_maze_app_release_versions</code> provider shown in
	<a class="text-accent" href="#versions-provider">Supplying dynamic options</a>. The callback only supplies
  the available choices; the field type determines whether the user can select one value or several.</p>

<h3 id="reading-multiple-selected-values">Reading multiple selected values</h3>
{{ codeblock.block('def _run_case(self, case: OrcraftTestCase):

	for tag in self.config.maze_app_versions:

		maze_app = MazeApp.from_release(
			tag,
			self.config.command,
			force=self.config.force_redownload,
		)

		maze_app.run()

	return case.result') }}
<p>The configuration value is a tuple of selected tags. <code>_run_case</code> loops over it, resolves each
  release through <code>MazeApp.from_release</code>, and runs the configured command once per version.</p>

<h2 id="matrix-and-cases">Matrix and Cases</h2>
<p><strong>Matrix and Cases</strong> generates one test case for every combination of values declared by the
	test's matrix. Select the test to see the generated cases below its regular configuration fields:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/advanced-configuration-parameters-group/matrix-and-cases-config.png", "Matrix and Cases test selected under the Advanced Configuration Parameters group, showing 24 selected cases generated from algorithm, seed, height and width dimensions", "large") }}
<p>The summary reports <strong>Total Cases: 24</strong>, and the table shows how each case was assembled. The
	<strong>algorithm</strong>, <strong>seed</strong>, <strong>height</strong> and <strong>width</strong> columns come
	directly from the matrix dimensions. <strong>Case</strong> is the generated case number, while
	<strong>Order</strong> is the position in which that case will run. With <strong>Run Order</strong> set to
	<strong>In Order</strong>, those values are the same.</p>
<p>Each check mark in <strong>Select</strong> includes that case in the run. Clearing one omits only that
	combination; it does not change the matrix definition. <strong>Case Cycles</strong> controls how many times
	each selected case runs, independently of the test-level <strong>Cycles</strong> value.</p>

<h3 id="defining-the-matrix">Defining the matrix</h3>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_advanced_configuration_parameters/test_matrix_and_cases/") }}
<p>Declare a <code>Matrix</code> field on the test config and add one <code>Dimension</code> for each case
	parameter. The first argument is the key used in the generated case; the second is the set of values that
	dimension can take:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('matrix = OrcraftConfigField.Matrix(
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension("algorithm", ("BFS", "DFS", "ASTAR")),
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension("seed", (42, 100)),
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension("height", (5, 10)),
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension("width", (15, 20)),
)') }}
<p>Orcraft builds the Cartesian product of those values. This matrix therefore creates
	<code>3 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 24</code> cases. In the default order, the last dimension changes fastest: width
	alternates first, followed by height, seed and then algorithm, which is the sequence visible in the table.</p>

<h3 id="using-matrix-values">Using matrix values</h3>
<p>Orcraft creates an <code>OrcraftTestCase</code> for each row in the Cartesian product, stores that row's
	dimension choices in the case's <code>params</code> mapping, and passes the case to the test's
	<code>_run_case</code> method.</p>
<p>For example, the first row in the screenshot produces a mapping equivalent to:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('{
	"algorithm": "BFS",
	"seed": 42,
	"height": 5,
	"width": 15,
}') }}
<p>The complete test class receives that generated case and uses its values to configure the Maze application:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('class TestMatrixAndCases(OrcraftTest):
	def __init__(self, config: TestMatrixAndCasesConfig | None = None) -> None:
		super().__init__(config or TestMatrixAndCasesConfig())

	def _run_case(self, case: OrcraftTestCase):

		maze_app = MazeApp(
			self.config.maze_executable_folder,
			"solve",
			width=case.params["width"],
			height=case.params["height"],
			seed=case.params["seed"],
			start_row=0,
			start_col=0,
			end_row=case.params["height"] - 1,
			end_col=case.params["width"] - 1,
			algorithm=case.params["algorithm"],
		)

		maze_app.run()

		return case.result') }}
<p>The <code>case: OrcraftTestCase</code> parameter is the framework interface for each generated combination.
	Every key used with <code>case.params</code> matches a dimension key in the matrix declaration above. Orcraft
	calls <code>_run_case</code> once for each selected combination, using the configured order and cycle counts.</p>